    `The book of Ruth opens with three losses in rapid succession — Naomi's husband, then her two sons, including Ruth's husband Mahlon. Three funerals. Two widows left in a foreign land. Naomi, the mother-in-law, releases her daughters-in-law to return to their own families, their own people, their own gods — and offers this release not as rejection but as the most honest love she has: go, find another husband, have a future. Orpah kisses her goodbye and returns. Ruth clings to her. The word clung — dabaq in Hebrew — is the same word used in Genesis 2:24 for the husband who leaves father and mother and clings to his wife. It is the word of covenant commitment, of a bond that holds even when the circumstances argue for its release. Ruth had every legitimate reason to leave. She had no legal obligation to stay. The covenant that had bound her to Naomi's household was dissolved by her husband's death. And she clung anyway. Where you go I will go. Where you stay I will stay. Your people will be my people and your God will be my God. This is not a romantic speech. It is a covenant declaration — the voluntary, costly, utterly unnecessary choice of a Moabite widow to bind herself to a destitute Israelite mother-in-law with no visible future and nothing to offer. This is hesed — the untranslatable Hebrew word for the covenant love that goes beyond what is required, that keeps faith when faith has not been earned, that remains loyal when loyalty has no remaining obligation. It is the word used of God's love for Israel across the entire Old Testament — the love that did not give up, that kept pursuing, that kept showing up with new mercies every morning even when Israel had done nothing to deserve new mercies. Ruth embodies hesed toward Naomi in exactly the way God embodies hesed toward His people. She is not merely a loyal daughter-in-law. She is a living parable of the character of God. Proverbs 31:10–31 describes a woman of noble character — the eshet chayil, the woman of valour — and the portrait that emerges is not primarily a domestic one, despite how the passage is often read. It is the portrait of a woman of extraordinary strength, generosity, wisdom, industry, and dignity who manages a household and a business and a community of dependents with a competence and a grace that produces blessing everywhere it reaches. Ruth embodies every quality in the list. She is industrious — she goes to the field and gleans from morning until evening without stopping. She is generous — she brings home not only what she has gleaned but the extra that Boaz has instructed his workers to leave for her, and she shares it with Naomi. She is courageous — she goes to the threshing floor at night, a vulnerable and potentially dangerous act, and presents herself to Boaz as a candidate for redemption. She is a woman of noble character, as Boaz himself declares in Ruth 3:11. She is clothed with strength and dignity. And she is a Moabite widow with no children and no property and no standing in the community she has chosen. The Proverbs 31 woman is not primarily a woman of impressive resources. She is a woman of impressive character — and character, the book of Ruth demonstrates with exquisite clarity, is available to everyone regardless of circumstance, origin, or the weight of loss that has preceded the moment of choosing.`
